# Line endings.
#
# Everything below is read by a Unix tool inside a container: /bin/sh execs the
# entrypoints, cron parses the crontab, Caddy parses the Caddyfile, and Compose
# parses the env file that scripts/bootstrap.sh derives from .env.example. All of
# them treat a trailing carriage return as part of the data.
#
# That matters because the Docker build context is not git-aware: `docker compose
# build` copies the working tree as it sits on disk. On a Windows checkout with
# core.autocrlf=true — the default that ships with Git for Windows — the tree is
# CRLF even though the committed blobs are LF, so the image gets `#!/bin/sh\r`
# and every container dies with
#
#     exec /app/compose/web/entrypoint.sh: no such file or directory
#
# which names the file it just failed to open and does not mention the real
# cause: /bin/sh\r is the interpreter that does not exist.
#
# eol=lf keeps these files LF in the working tree on every platform. Source code
# is left to text=auto, so editors on Windows keep native endings there.

* text=auto

*.sh            text eol=lf
*.cron          text eol=lf
Caddyfile       text eol=lf
.env.example    text eol=lf
